name bingo-abort-1
timeout 300

# THE MINIMUM-FUEL DECISION, and the first mission in this tree in which a fuel state changes anything.
# The geometry is bvr-intercept.fbm's, unchanged: 99 km head-on, co-altitude, one AIM-120 shooter
# against a bandit that never looks, never shoots and never manoeuvres. TWO lines differ from that file
# and they are the whole experiment:
#
#   set fuel_int_pct 60     4,183 lb, INTERNAL only — a clean jet, declarable for the first time
#                           (`fuel_pct` would also have fuelled two external tanks this jet is not
#                           carrying; doc/modules/stores.md §Gaps 3)
#   set brief_bingo_lbs 6000  the number this pilot commits to. 4,183 < 6,000, so the warning stands
#                           from the moment the entry reaches the DED
#
# WHAT USED TO HAPPEN AND WHY IT DID NOT: `FBPilot::CanPressOn` is the only line in the pilot that reads
# the BINGO warning, and it sat behind a branch the general one overtook one tick after the defence
# window ended — measured on w3-06-bingo.fbm as the bit set for 5,200 of 5,200 rows against a
# byte-identical `eng_state` column. The fuel question is now asked where it can be reached: below
# survival and below an unfinished missile support, above everything that is a statement about the
# picture rather than about the aeroplane (doc/pilot.md §7.4a).
#
# WHAT TO READ, and what it measured:
#   events.log   `t=4.1 INFO intercept BINGO_ABORT fuelLbs=4174.3 bingoLbs=6000 from=search haveTgt=0`
#                — once, with the fuel, the threshold and the state it left
#   eng_state    search -> abort at t = 4.1 s, and it stays there: a jet that turns cold does not
#                come back. bingo-press.fbm, one line away: search -> closing (52.0) -> attack (140.1)
#                -> support (147.9) -> attack (172.5)
#   the track    heading 90.0 deg -> 230.4 deg by t = 60 s -> 263.5 deg at the end; the jet finishes
#                65 km WEST of the point it was spawned at, having never been engaged
#   warn_active  2 (`FBWarnBingo`) here, 0 in bingo-press.fbm — the only bit that differs between them
#
# READING RULE: exit 3 (TIMEOUT) is the CORRECT outcome. The `objective kill unit bandit` is deliberately
# kept from bvr-intercept.fbm so that the file states plainly what the decision COSTS — a pilot who goes
# home at minimum fuel does not kill anybody, and the campaign that briefs the number is buying exactly
# that. Read beside bingo-press.fbm, which is this file with the one bingo line deleted; alone it proves
# nothing.

unit viper
  team friendly
  module f16
  spawn 46.90000 6.40000 8000 90.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_int_pct 60                      # 4,183 lb internal, external tanks empty
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode crm                         # search, no auto-lock: the pilot decides when to lock
  set store 3 aim120
  set store 7 aim120
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set brief_bingo_lbs 6000                 # above the fuel aboard: BINGO from the first DED entry
  set task intercept
  wp 46.90000 8.35000 8000 450             # the controller's vector: threat bearing 090, co-altitude

  objective kill unit bandit
unit bandit
  team hostile
  module f16
  spawn 46.90000 7.70000 8000 270.0 400
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode off                         # it does not look, and therefore never warns anyone
  set rwr off
  wp 46.90000 5.90000 8000 400             # a straight westbound leg through the engagement
